when ebola hit the eastern part of the  drc in 2019  aid workers many from the world health  organization were supposed to be saving  lives  but reports have emerged that some  allegedly offered jobs in exchange for  sex  exploiting women who needed work over 70  women accused the humanitarians of  sexual exploitation  sexual assault and even rape dw's east  africa correspondent marielle muller  sent us this report about new cases that  have emerged from the city of bhutembo  the two women whose accounts were about  to hear did not want to be fully  identified  desanja has to be mother to those her  sister left behind  she blames a wh who doctor for her  sister pandesa's death  that doctor promised my sister a job  saying that they would be working  together  and that is when my sister pandesa got  pregnant even before she started working  with that the problem began he turned  his phone off  pendesa needed to hide her situation  from her husband  who was working away from home she  decided to have an abortion  that's risky in drc and things  went wrong  she went to the hospital at that time we  could not accompany her because of ebola  when she got there she did not even  spend the night they didn't treat her  and she was taken to the ebola camp  the doctors assumed her bleeding was  ebola not a botched abortion  she died that night and was buried like  an ebola victim  isolated from her family her husband  never returned  disorge struggles to provide for the  three children  she blames the who worker who abandoned  her sister  he should be arrested  this woman's story is similar but she  survived  we'll call her masika he told me he was  a doctor  working for the ebola response team  she had three children and no job so she  took his offer of work as a cleaner  in exchange for sex but he got her  pregnant  and pressured her to have an abortion  i got the abortion because it was a  difficult situation in my life  he told me i did not come here to build  a family but to work  for me it was a situation of life and  death  the doctor blocked her calls her family  threw her out  now she's rebuilding her life alone  this group is breaking a taboo by  talking about how contacts between eight  workers  and local women can end they say it's  sexual exploitation  an abuse of wealth and power  now a woman knows that a humanitarian is  not allowed to abuse her  she has a helpline she can call if the  person is not punished the woman can be  psychologically troubled  and can't return to her normal life  the who has launched an investigation  into media reports of dozens of  exploitative relationships between its  staff  and local women it said it could not  talk with us until the investigation is  complete  disorg says her whole family was  betrayed by those  sent to help who  should take steps so that others can see  it and they don't repeat this stupid  behavior  because these are serious issues  for more on this story we've invited  journalist robert flumerfeldt  onto the show he joins us now from  bukavu  south kibel province in drc welcome to  dw news  africa robert you worked on the story  as well you broke the story actually how  did you  discover these abuse cases  well um myself and a number of other  journalists who had been working  in the zone of the ebola response pretty  close to since the beginning i had been  talking to community members about  reasons for their resentment toward the  ebola response people would describe  things like militarization and  corruption  but a common refrain that a lot of  people heard was that oh you know the  responders would come in with all their  money and they treat us all like  prostitutes you know they come in  and they just want us for sex and things  like this and so when i began working  with  the new humanitarian thompson reuters  foundation and a team of journalists  like anchika song gosiifa bahati sam  mednick  we worked with trusted um local civil  society  sources and researchers to begin a sort  of trust building process with a number  of individual victims and indeed  individual victims were apprehensive for  quite a long period about talking to us  understandably so but after we talked to  the first people who described being  victimized in this way  um the women would say well actually i  know a lot of other people in my  workplace were hired like this who had  sex demanded of them before they were  employed  and so thus we sort of tapped into a  network  of victims who have shared experiences  or similar experiences and  we're able to build trust for them like  this and and what did you find in your  investigation  well we found really um across the two  investigations that we published  we spoke to more than 70 women  approximately 75 women  who described really horrific forms of  sexual exploitation and abuse  widespread practice especially in who  and other un agencies like unicef and  iom  of demanding sex in exchange for jobs a  number of instances of  women describing being raped a number of  instances of women describing  contracting venereal disease  diseases being impregnated um one  particular  particularly horrific instance of a  woman dying after  a dangerous sponged abortion a number of  instances of women being forced to seek  abortions from the individuals who  impregnated them  and overwhelmingly these individuals  being people working with the world  health organization  a number of other agencies a lot of  foreigners and so forth but people work  in the  working in the ebola response yeah right  um just  going into these cases let's look first  at for example  there were cases alleging rape  why didn't these women take these up uh  you know as legal  or criminal cases well um in particular  with respect to rape um  it's particularly horrific to not  dynamic but  for instance one of the women that we  spoke to was raped and after she was  raped she still hoped that she would be  employed  by who and indeed she was um and so that  sort of economic desperation  was certainly always a dynamic um  there's additionally the issue of the  fact that just generally speaking  um the sort of legal system in congo is  not especially effective and in  particular with respect to sexual  violence um sort of particularly  egregious and finally and most  importantly um women were very  apprehensive about going public because  of concerns about stigma from community  members in a number of cases women  hadn't even told their husbands  that they had been abused like this and  didn't want to start a protracted legal  process  and likewise feared facing um  retribution or  threats or something like this from the  people who had abused them in the first  place  in in our report that we we heard  earlier there was  desanj who is struggling to look after  her dead sister's  three children what chance do those  children have of getting any  compensation from  uh the who worker or even from the who  itself  you know unfortunately your guess is as  good as mine uh what we know  is what who has made public after our  initial investigation was published last  september  there was a media storm and who came  forward made the sort of standard  rhetorical commitments to zero tolerance  and said it would engage in  robust investigations after that period  eight months lapsed and it seemed that  there weren't any investigations  occurring on the ground eight to nine  months  later in may or june a team began  conducting investigations of what was  going on it's not clear what was  happening in any intervening period  and that team that is doing the work  it's not clear what their methodology is  it's not clear whether they would be who  would be willing to provide compensation  or psychological services to victims  none of this had been made has been made  clear there's been a total lack of  transparency  on w.h.o zen okay  journalist robert flummerfeldt in  bukharvu south kivu province in the drc  thank you very much for  explaining that to us
 

